## Who this guidance is for

Follow this guidance when accessing DfE GitHub organisations, contributing to DfE repositories or using DfE
funded GitHub Copilot. It explains how to prepare your account, authenticate through single sign on, protect
credentials and work safely with code.

This guide combines official GitHub guidance with proposed DfE working requirements. DfE specific requirements
need approval before publication. Follow existing approved DfE policies and the rules for your repository
where these impose additional controls.

### Your essential responsibilities

- Use the GitHub account approved for your DfE work and authenticate with your own DfE Microsoft Entra
  identity. Never share an account.

- Add and verify your education.gov.uk email address. Use the approved DfE address for work commits and
  notifications as required by DfE policy.

- Complete the required security and GitHub training, and Copilot training before using the funded service.
  Ask for clarification if you do not understand a requirement.

- Keep credentials out of repositories, issues, pull requests and AI prompts. Follow the required review,
  testing and security checks.

- Report access problems, exposed credentials and suspected misuse through the appropriate support or security
  route.

### What SSO does

Single sign on links your GitHub account to your DfE identity. When SAML SSO is enforced, members must
authenticate through the identity provider to access the organisation’s protected resources. Microsoft Entra
ID may require MFA or other checks under DfE policy. Repository access still depends on your assigned
permissions.

GitHub account security, DfE authentication, repository permissions and email verification are separate
controls. A successful SSO sign in does not grant access to every repository or replace secure development
practices.

### Account ownership

Where GitHub personal accounts are linked to SAML, they remain personal accounts in GitHub’s technical account
model. A work email and SSO do not convert an account into an Enterprise Managed User. Use only the account
DfE has approved for work; do not assume an existing account used for personal projects is approved. Separate
work account and contractor exceptions must follow the approved DfE process.

## Prepare your account and sign in

### Set up your work identity

- Check that you can sign into your DfE Microsoft Entra account and use the required MFA method.

- In GitHub Settings, open Emails, add your own education.gov.uk address and complete verification using the
  email GitHub sends. Set the DfE address as the primary email on a dedicated DfE work account, subject to
  approved local policy.

- Check your Git commit identity in each work repository. Set your DfE name and email locally so personal
  projects are not changed unintentionally. Verify the result before committing.

- Use a strong account authentication method and enable GitHub 2FA in line with organisation policy. Prefer
  phishing resistant methods such as passkeys or security keys where supported and approved. Store recovery
  information securely.

Example for a work repository terminal:

```shell
git config user.name "Your Name"
git config user.email "your.name@education.gov.uk"
git config --get user.email
```

A commit email is attribution metadata, not proof of authentication or legal ownership. Apply DfE intellectual
property and employment or contract requirements separately.

### Sign in through SSO

1. Sign into the approved GitHub account. If you have several browser sessions, check the username before
  proceeding.

1. Open the organisation’s SSO link supplied through an official DfE communication or use the SSO prompt shown
  by GitHub.

1. Follow the redirect to Microsoft Entra ID, select your DfE work identity and complete the required
  authentication checks.

1. Check that you are linking the correct GitHub account. If the identity is already linked to a different
  account, stop and contact support.

1. Return to GitHub and confirm access to a repository you are authorised to use. Test your normal Git and IDE
  workflow as well.

### Sessions and access changes

GitHub may ask you to authenticate again when an SSO session expires. Use the same approved identity. Before a
communicated enforcement deadline, complete sign in and report failures promptly. Members who have not
authenticated through the IdP may be removed when enforcement is enabled.

If a repository is missing, ask the owner or support team to check membership, team permissions and SSO. Do
not create a second account or request administrator access merely to work around the problem.

## Use Git credentials and tools securely

### Choose the appropriate authentication method

Browser sign in and Git authentication are different. Use an approved credential manager, SSH key or token for
Git and API access. Do not use your account password for HTTPS Git operations. Ask Platform Engineering which
method is approved for your use case.

| Method | What you need to do |
| --- | --- |
| Classic personal access token | Where permitted, authorise it for the organisation through Configure SSO after signing in through the IdP. Limit scopes and expiry. |
| Fine grained personal access token | Select only the required resource owner, repositories and permissions. Complete creation and any organisation approval process; SSO authorisation happens during creation. |
| SSH key | Use an approved key, protect the private key and authorise the public key for the organisation through Configure SSO. |
| IDE or GitHub CLI | Sign into the approved account and complete any SSO or application authorisation prompts. Check permissions and credential state if access fails. |

Once an account has a linked SAML identity, credential authorisation can be required even while SSO
enforcement is off. Signing into the browser alone may not resolve an unauthorised token or key.

### Protect credentials

- Grant only the access needed, use expiry dates and revoke credentials that are lost, exposed or no longer
  needed. Follow DfE’s approved credential storage process.

- Do not put tokens, passwords, connection strings or private keys in code, Git history, configuration
  committed to Git, screenshots, chat messages or AI prompts.

- Do not share a user token or password with a colleague. Each person must have their own identity and access
  approval.

- For unattended integrations, ask Platform Engineering to select an approved GitHub App or workload identity
  design. Do not build shared production automation around your personal login without review.

### If a secret is exposed

Report it immediately through the DfE security incident route and arrange revocation or rotation with the
credential owner. Deleting the visible text does not invalidate the secret or remove it from history. Preserve
evidence and follow the agreed remediation process. Do not attempt a force push or history rewrite without
coordination.

## Get help and keep access current

### Troubleshoot access in order

- Confirm your GitHub username, organisation and DfE identity are correct.

- Use the organisation SSO link to renew authentication. Check your DfE sign in and MFA work.

- For Git or API failures, check the relevant key or token’s expiry, permissions, SSO authorisation and
  approval status. Do not increase permissions indiscriminately.

- Check with the repository owner whether membership or team access has changed.

- Contact support with the details below if the issue persists. Do not unlink your SAML identity, disable
  security controls or repeatedly create new credentials.

| Issue | Include in your request |
| --- | --- |
| Browser SSO or MFA failure | Organisation, username, time, error text and Entra correlation ID if shown. |
| Git or IDE access failure | Repository, tool, operation, sanitised error and credential type. Never include the credential itself. |
| Missing repository or permissions | Repository, required task, team and approving service owner. |
| Suspected compromise or exposed secret | Use the DfE security incident process immediately; provide relevant evidence without disclosing live secrets. |

Proposed internal support contact: Platform.ENGINEERING@education.gov.uk. Confirm the mailbox
is the published support route before release. It is internal use only. External collaborators must use their
agreed sponsor or external support channel. Never send recovery codes or authentication secrets to this
mailbox.

### When your role changes or you leave

- Notify the access owner when access is no longer required, your role changes or your contract ends. Follow
  the approved offboarding process.

- Hand over repository ownership, open work and automation dependencies before leaving. Do not transfer
  credentials by email or chat.

- Administrators must coordinate identity assignments, GitHub membership, sessions and credentials. Users
  should not assume that disabling an Entra account removes every possible access path.

